# Line endings.
#
# Every shell script in this repository is executed on a Linux or macOS runner:
# scripts/check-shared-drift.sh, macos/Scripts/*.sh, and the shell integration
# templates in shared/shell-integration/. A CRLF in any of them is fatal there,
# and the error does not name the real cause: the kernel reads the shebang as
# `/usr/bin/env bash\r` and reports that bash does not exist, or the shell reads
# `set -euo pipefail\r` and rejects an option nobody typed.
#
# Whether that happens today depends on a setting on the contributor's machine,
# which is not somewhere correctness should live. `git config core.autocrlf` is
# usually `true` on Windows, which converts CRLF back to LF on the way into a
# commit and hides the problem. When it is `false`, or when a file is added by a
# tool that writes CRLF, the CRLF goes straight into the blob and CI breaks for
# everyone.
#
# The rules below take that decision away from the machine. `text=auto` lets git
# normalise anything it detects as text, and the explicit `eol=lf` entries pin
# the files where the ending is not a preference but a requirement.

* text=auto

*.sh    text eol=lf
*.bash  text eol=lf
*.zsh   text eol=lf
*.fish  text eol=lf
*.yml   text eol=lf
*.yaml  text eol=lf
Makefile text eol=lf

# .ps1 is deliberately NOT pinned to CRLF, even though it only ever runs on
# Windows. shared/shell-integration/zharp.ps1 is compared byte for byte against
# the copy embedded in the app source by scripts/check-shared-drift.sh, which
# runs on an Ubuntu runner. Checking the shared copy out with CRLF there while
# the embedded literal has LF would read as drift on every single pull request.
# PowerShell does not care either way.

# Binary, so git never tries to diff, merge or convert them. Most of these are
# the brand package under shared/.
*.png   binary
*.jpg   binary
*.ico   binary
*.icns  binary
*.ttf   binary
*.dmg   binary
*.exe   binary
